<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.6.4: Solving Equations Using the Quadratic Formula</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02432</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.6.4: Solving Equations Using the Quadratic Formula</md:title>
    <md:uuid>492926a3-a57c-483e-b072-639352d3e6a9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">Here is the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">quadratic formula</term>:</para>
<para id="para-00002">\(x=\frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2- 4ac}}{2a}\)</para>
<example id="exam-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00003">Use the formula to solve the equation \(x^2+9x+8=0\).</para>
  
  <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00006">The solutions are -8 and -1.</para>
  
  <para id="para-00007">Apply the quadratic formula for \(a=1\), \(b=9\), \(c=8\):</para>
  
  <para id="para-00008">\(x=\frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{(9)^2- 4(1)(8)}}{2(1)}=\frac{-9 \pm \sqrt{49}}{2}\), which means</para>
  
  <para id="para-00009">\(x=\frac{-9-7}{2}=-8\) and \(x=\frac{-9+7}{2}=-1\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
